    "lawCode": "Amd §11-0535, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8750\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nremoval of species from the endangered and threatened species list\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo protect endangered and threatened species that still require such\nprotection in New York in the event that the Department of Interior\nremoves its endangered or threatened designation.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill amends subdivision 1 of section 11-0535 of the\nenvironmental conservation law, as amended by chapter 706 of the laws of\n2005 to require any endangered and threatened species, as designated by\nthe\n\nSecretary of the Interior, to continue to be subject to the restrictions\nof section 11-0535, regardless of removal of such designation as an\nendangered or threatened species by the U.S.\n\nSecretary of the Interior.\n\nSection 2 establishes the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSince the enactment of New York State's Endangered Species Act in 1970,\nthe State has recognized the protection of fish and wildlife as a matter\nof pressing state concern. It is the duty of the State to take action to\nassure the continued survival of its endangered species of fish and\nwildlife and their critical habitat.\n\nUnder the current structure of New York statute, species that are\nafforded protection as a result of designation by the United States\nDepartment of Interior's Fish and Wildlife Service, independent of State\naction, are vulnerable to decisions made at the federal level.  For\nexample, in the case where the Secretary removes endangered and threat-\nened designation for species that do not have specific state desig-\nnation, state law affords no continuing protection. A number of New York\nspecies that are listed as endangered or threatened are currently\nprotected based solely on their federal designation. If the Secretary of\nthe Interior were to delist these species, they would no longer be\nprotected in New York, placing their populations and critical habitat in\njeopardy. It is critical that once a species is afforded protection in\nNew York State, that only the State itself has the authority to extend\nor remove these protections based on its own investigations, and the\nbest interests of the fish and wildlife within its borders.\n\nThis bill would extend the authority of the commissioner to protect\nendangered or threatened species it so chooses, that are at risk of\nlosing protection within New York State as a result of action by the\nU.S. Secretary of the Interior.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2020: 55196 passed the Senate. 2019: S5196 passed the Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
